# <type>(<scope>): <imperative subject, 50 characters or fewer>
#
# Describe the changes using concise imperative bullet points.
#
# Why: <maintainer-supplied reason; omit for a trivial change>
#
# Types: docs, fix, update, style, ci.
# Other useful types: chore, feat, refactor.
#
# Scopes: readme, policy-template, agents-template, disclosure-template,
# readme-template, contributing-template, pull-request-template,
# gitmessage-template, git, github, license.
# Pick one dominant scope or omit it. This vocabulary is preferred, not required.
#
# For a breaking change, add ! after the type or scope and explain it in the body.
#
# Add each applicable trailer as an uncommented line. All trailers sit in one
# final paragraph, one per line, with no blank line between them and nothing
# after them; Why: stays in the body above that paragraph. Repeat Assisted-by
# for multiple materially involved tools or models.
#
# Assisted-by: <tool>, <model identifier or not recorded> (<role or extent>)
# Checks-run: <check actually run> — <observed result>
# Ground-truth-source: <independent source for an added or changed reference value>
#
# Roles: plan, partial implementation, full implementation, review, refactor.
#
# Omit Checks-run when no check ran.
# Omit Ground-truth-source when no reference value changed.
# A property test does not require a Ground-truth-source trailer.
#
# Record materially agent-assisted commits with Assisted-by, including prose edits.
# Author-edited prose is disclosed at the file or release level in the README.
#
# Git uses this template only for editor-based commits when commit.template is set.
# git commit -m bypasses it. Review the final message, including squash commits.